1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,306
In 1901, a robbery that failed still broke

2
00:00:03,316 --> 00:00:04,477
a bank in Wanatah.

3
00:00:05,027 --> 00:00:07,800
It was the Wanatah Bank, owned by one man,

4
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:08,787
Julius Conitz.

5
00:00:09,017 --> 00:00:10,989
That fall, someone tried to rob it, and

6
00:00:10,999 --> 00:00:11,436
failed.

7
00:00:11,986 --> 00:00:13,537
But the attempt frightened many

8
00:00:13,547 --> 00:00:14,228
depositors.

9
00:00:14,458 --> 00:00:16,167
They rushed to take their money out.

10
00:00:16,617 --> 00:00:19,212
On December 12, the bank closed its doors.

11
00:00:19,862 --> 00:00:23,772
On paper, Conitz had $91,000 in property,

12
00:00:23,782 --> 00:00:25,230
three times what was owed.

13
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:28,268
But $40,000 of it was railroad stock.

14
00:00:28,818 --> 00:00:30,771
One paper reported he couldn't turn it

15
00:00:30,781 --> 00:00:33,023
into cash fast enough, so he signed

16
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:35,747
everything he owned over to trustees, even

17
00:00:35,757 --> 00:00:37,387
his three farms and his home.

18
00:00:37,937 --> 00:00:39,034
The robbery failed.

19
00:00:39,264 --> 00:00:40,992
Two weeks later, so did the bank.

20
00:00:41,742 --> 00:00:43,786
Comment your town, and it could be next.

